Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  UpdatedType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 05  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 05  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 Frozen Section Pathologist  None Frozen Section Resident  None Frozen Section Consultant s   None Consulting Pathologist s   None Assigned Resident Path  Paula Arnell  M D  Reports To  BARBARA LYNN SMITH  M D             Addended Report             Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results CLINICAL DATA  Right needle localization  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISION  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  TUBULAR AND MUCINOUS TYPES  SEE TABLE  INVASIVE LOBULAR CARCINOMA  SEE TABLE  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  SEE TABLE  LOBULAR NEOPLASIA  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU  ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH USUAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA  HEALING BIOPSY SITE  TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS INVASIVE CARCINOMA Tumor size  Three foci  REMOVED_DATE x 0 25cm  invasive ductal  tubular type  See note  REMOVED_DATE x 0 15cm  invasive ductal  mucinous type  See note  REMOVED_DATE x 0 45cm  invasive lobular  See note  Grade  1  tubular and mucinous types  and 2  lobular  Lymphatic vessel invasion  Not identified Blood vessel invasion  Not identified  Margin of invasive carcinoma  Invasive carcinoma extends to less than 0 1cm from the antero superior  lobular type   inferior and posterior  mucinous type  margins of the specimen focally  Note  A cytokeratin stain confirms the presence of tumor close to the antero superior margin  Location of ductal carcinoma in situ  Within and slightly beyond the region of the mass Grade of ductal carcinoma in situ  1 Margin of ductal carcinoma in situ  The distances to all margins measure 0 2cm or greater from all definite in situ carcinoma  Stains for receptors   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   lobular  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   tubular and mucinous types  NOTE  The three foci of invasive carcinoma are found in the same general vicinity  however  they exhibit three different morphologies  mucinous  tubular  and lobular configurations  The mucinous carcinoma is associated with a mucinous in situ component  The tubular carcinoma has an associated non mucinous in situ component  while the lobular is associated with lobular carcinoma in situ  These findings would argue for three separate primary tumors  The lobular carcinoma component does exhibit rare tubule like formation  and the majority  but not all  of the invasive tumor cells demonstrate membranous staining with e cadherin antibody  suggesting that it could be classified along with the tubular component as a tubulolobular carcinoma  Dr  Koerner has reviewed selected slides and concurs with this interpretation  The invasive nature of the tubular carcinoma is confirmed with stains for myoepithelial cells  calponin  p63  and myosin   BREAST  RIGHT  FINAL SHAVED MEDIAL 12 00 3 00 MARGIN   EXCISION  NO IN SITU OR INVASIVE CARCINOMA IS IDENTIFIED  NOTE  A cytokeratin immunoperoxidase stain helps support that a focus of crushed cells is stromal rather than epithelial  BREAST  RIGHT  FINAL SHAVED MEDIAL 3 00 6 00 MARGIN   EXCISION  NO DEFINITE IN SITU OR INVASIVE CARCINOMA IS IDENTIFIED  BREAST  RIGHT  FINAL SHAVED LATERAL HALF OF CAVITY MARGIN   EXCISION  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  GRADE REMOVED_DATE  ONE MICROSCOPIC FOCUS  SPANNING APPROXIMATELY 0 55CM  LOBULAR NEOPLASIA  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU  NOTE  The presence of both in situ ductal and lobular carcinoma is confirmed by an immunohistochemical stain for e cadherin  LYMPH NODE  RIGHT AXILLA   SENTINEL    EXCISION  METASTATIC CARCINOMA IN ONE OUT OF ONE LYMPH NODE  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The metastatic tumor glands aggregate to approximately 0 18cm  micrometastasis  and are identified on both the HE and cytokeratin immunoperoxidase stained slides  No extracapsular extension is identified  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PAT